[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the HTTP application layer on the internal web server. The Apache HTTP Server (apache2) daemon on srv1 is stopped, so no web service is available on port 80.

Mechanism: Client PCs resolve campus names correctly to 10.5.0.4 (srv1) via DNS, but when they try to connect over HTTP the TCP connection fails because nothing is listening on port 80. Browsers often summarize this as “site can’t be found,” which can be mistaken for a DNS issue.

Localization: srv1 (10.5.0.4, interface eth0) hosts both DNS and the web content for the jeremysitlab.com campus zone. Evidence:
- DNS is healthy: from pc1, getent hosts srv1.jeremysitlab.com and www.jeremysitlab.com both return 10.5.0.4; ping to 10.5.0.4 succeeds; srv1 is listening on UDP/TCP 53 and has an authoritative zone with wildcard A -> 10.5.0.4 (/etc/bind/db.jeremysitlab).
- HTTP is down: curl http://srv1.jeremysitlab.com and http://10.5.0.4 return HTTP “000” (no response), and srv1 shows no listeners on 80/443. systemctl reports apache2.service “Active: inactive (dead).”

Root cause: The apache2 service on srv1 was not running, leaving no web server to serve internal sites.

Corrective fix: Start and enable Apache on srv1 (systemctl start apache2; systemctl enable apache2) and verify with ss/netstat that port 80 is listening and curl returns 200. Review Apache logs/config to understand why it stopped and add monitoring/alerts to prevent recurrence.
=== END ===